<clickhouse>
  <!--
    The metrics scraper's principal (Phase 18a). Identity lives in users.d, like
    agent-ro.xml and allow-network.xml, and is reconstructed from this file at every
    container start — it cannot drift the way a SQL-created user in the mutable
    access/ store can.

    `metrics_ro` is granted SELECT on exactly TWO system tables (`system.parts`,
    `system.merges`) plus SHOW TABLES on the five tables the scraper counts. It cannot read a single row of pipeline data — not
    attributed_conversions, not exposures_landed, nothing in `default` — which is the
    point: the scraper reports how ClickHouse is storing the data, never what the data
    says. Pinned by tests/integration/test_metrics_ro.py (the mirror of the agent_ro
    write-denied test, SN2).

    Grant form, NOT <readonly>1</readonly>: readonly=1 can reject the benign session
    settings clickhouse-connect sends on connect (400), whereas a SELECT grant is
    enforced at the access-control layer and leaves the session alone. Inline <grants>
    is supported since ClickHouse 22.4 (image is 24.8).

    `agent_ro`'s grants are UNCHANGED by this phase — a second principal, not a wider
    first one.

    Passwordless, ::/0, same local-dev posture as agent-ro.xml: the host publishes 8123
    on 127.0.0.1 only (compose), so the service stays off the LAN. Not for
    shared/multi-user hosts (BACKLOG). No secret is committed; a hardened deploy points
    CLICKHOUSE_METRICS_USER / _PASSWORD at its own credential, which lives in .env and
    never in CI.
  -->
  <users>
    <metrics_ro>
      <password></password>
      <networks replace="replace">
        <ip>::/0</ip>
      </networks>
      <grants>
        <query>GRANT SELECT ON system.parts</query>
        <query>GRANT SELECT ON system.merges</query>
        <!--
          ClickHouse FILTERS the rows of system.parts / system.merges to the tables the
          user may see: without a grant on `default`, the scrape returns zero rows and
          silently reports "0 active parts" (found live, Phase 18a — ARCHITECTURE §8).
          SHOW TABLES lifts that filter. It is granted PER TABLE, on exactly the five
          the scraper enumerates (observability/ch_scrape.py TABLES) — not on
          `default.*`, so `eval_meta`, `rollup_refreshed` and every table a later phase
          adds (including the migration's `report_snapshots_v2` scratch) stay invisible
          here rather than becoming visible by default.

          What SHOW confers and what it does not: table names, and — through
          system.tables — engine and sort key; NOT a row of data. Every SELECT, INSERT,
          ALTER, DROP and CREATE against a pipeline table is ACCESS_DENIED, pinned by
          tests/integration/test_metrics_ro.py. It also confers CHECK TABLE, which
          checksums a table's parts: read amplification available to a metadata reader,
          stated rather than hidden (security review, Phase 18a).
        -->
        <query>GRANT SHOW TABLES ON default.attributed_conversions</query>
        <query>GRANT SHOW TABLES ON default.exposures_landed</query>
        <query>GRANT SHOW TABLES ON default.campaign_hourly</query>
        <query>GRANT SHOW TABLES ON default.report_snapshots</query>
        <query>GRANT SHOW TABLES ON default.rollup_dirty</query>
      </grants>
    </metrics_ro>
  </users>
</clickhouse>
